group-sync-dashboard deployed to namespace group-sync-dashboard.

Image: image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000/group-sync-dashboard/group-sync-dashboard:2.0.0-708e6be104

Route (the router names the host and reports it in status; spec.host is empty unless you set it):
  oc get route group-sync-dashboard -n group-sync-dashboard -o jsonpath='{.status.ingress[0].host}'

Confirm what is actually running — the pod reports the commit it was built from:
  curl -sk https://<the host above>/api/version

The KPI page's doors and the Grafana board (chart README, "Prerequisites — the Grafana and Observe
integration"): user-workload monitoring on (the OpenShift engineers, once per cluster; the
openshift-grafana chart's post-install Job reports it); a Grafana in this namespace —
  discovered by the Route labelled app.kubernetes.io/name=openshift-grafana in group-sync-dashboard (the openshift-grafana
  chart's: helm install grafana <repo>/openshift-grafana -n group-sync-dashboard --timeout 15m);
  until one exists the page says so instead of drawing a dead button
and `view` on group-sync-dashboard for every reader group (the console's namespace dashboard and
Grafana's login both check `get pods` here):
  oc adm policy add-role-to-group view <group> -n group-sync-dashboard

Authentication: OpenShift OAuth. Anyone who can log into this cluster can view the
dashboard.
Sessions: the proxy cookie lives 4h from login, and it is ABSOLUTE — there is
no cookie refresh and no mid-session revalidation, so an active reader is signed out at
that mark too, and a revoked user keeps access until it. Change it with
oauthProxy.cookie.expire. Signing out ends the proxy session; it does NOT revoke the OAuth
token, because this chart authenticates as a ServiceAccount whose tokens carry only
user:info and user:check-access — the DELETE was built, measured returning 403, and dropped.

Visibility: RESTRICTED (visibility.enabled=true, the default). Each reader sees only their
own profile, groups, grants and login attempts. A reader sees everything only if a
SubjectAccessReview allows:

  list clusterrolebindings.rbac.authorization.k8s.io

This is a custom check (visibility.adminSar): whoever holds a role granting it gets the
wide view. Stock cluster-reader passes every threshold measured for this feature.
Grant the wide view through normal RBAC, never a chart value:

  oc adm policy add-cluster-role-to-user cluster-reader <username>

  (prefer the group form — a grant naming a person survives their departure:
   oc adm policy add-cluster-role-to-group cluster-reader <ldap-group>)

An indeterminate review (API error, timeout) yields the narrow view, never the wide one.
Restore the old everyone-sees-everything behaviour only as a deliberate choice:
  --set visibility.enabled=false

Several clusters are configured. The reader was authenticated by this pod's OWN cluster; what
they see about each of the others is that entry's own policy (docs/ACCESS_CONTROL.md §11):
  dashboard: visibility inherit (host), identity same-as-host (host)
  walk-self-login: visibility remote-sar (default), identity same-as-host (default)

Login capture: AUDIT LOG. The dashboard reads /var/log/oauth-server/audit.log on the
control-plane nodes through the API server's node proxy — a ClusterRole with `get nodes/proxy`,
which is read access to everything the kubelet serves on those nodes. No Debug verbosity is
needed. Clusters left at Debug must be restored manually; see the chart README migration note.
First read backfills as far as the rotated audit files reach, 400 days at most.

Reporting is ON: the report service runs as deploy/group-sync-dashboard-report on
image-registry.openshift-image-registry.svc:5000/group-sync-dashboard/group-sync-dashboard-report:2.0.0-708e6be104, reached through the dashboard's proxy under /report/. Administrators
(the wide tier) generate reports from the Reports tab; the Usage tab records who generated what.
Pre-flight for TLS between the pods (reporting.tls.enabled):
  oc exec -n group-sync-dashboard deploy/group-sync-dashboard -c oauth-proxy -- /usr/bin/oauth-proxy --help 2>&1 | grep upstream-ca
